## Pinning & Lifecycle

A realm consumes a bundle by **pinning** a checksum into its `manifest.beam.json` `references` (`@<alias>/<name>` → `sha256:…`). Pins are what actually get deployed. `bundles pin` resolves a checksum (from a tag by default, or an exact one) and writes it.
```
beam_exec("bundles pin checkout-stack -q")
```
{{ if commands["bundles pin"] }}{{ if (array.size commands["bundles pin"].arguments) > 0 }}
#### `beam bundles pin` arguments
| Argument | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
{{ for arg in commands["bundles pin"].arguments }}| `{{ arg.name }}` | {{ arg.type }} | {{ arg.description }} |
{{ end }}{{ end }}{{ if (array.size commands["bundles pin"].options) > 0 }}
#### `beam bundles pin` options
| Option | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
{{ for opt in commands["bundles pin"].options }}| `{{ opt.name }}` | {{ opt.type }} | {{ opt.description }} |
{{ end }}{{ end }}{{ end }}

**Yank** retires a checksum so it can no longer be *newly* referenced. Crucially, yank **does not** break existing pins — an already-pinned yanked checksum still redeploys. Yank requires the exact `@sha256:…` reference.
```
beam_exec("bundles yank checkout-stack@sha256:abc123… -q")
```
{{ if commands["bundles yank"] }}{{ if (array.size commands["bundles yank"].arguments) > 0 }}
#### `beam bundles yank` arguments
